WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.640
Movie, My Cat in the Box.

00:02.640 --> 00:06.880
Have you ever wondered what happens to a cat in a box when you're not looking at it?

00:06.880 --> 00:11.960
In the fascinating world of quantum physics, we encounter the intriguing idea of Schrödinger's

00:11.960 --> 00:12.960
cat.

00:12.960 --> 00:13.960
Picture this.

00:13.960 --> 00:18.960
A feline, in a sealed box, could be both alive and dead at the same time.

00:18.960 --> 00:20.360
It's a mind-bending concept, isn't it?

00:20.360 --> 00:24.760
This peculiar situation hinges on a previous random event that hasn't been observed.

00:24.760 --> 00:27.320
Now, that's a curious thought, isn't it?

00:27.320 --> 00:32.640
Imagine a cat, a flask of poison, and a radioactive source all put inside a sealed box.

00:32.640 --> 00:34.840
Now, here's where things get interesting.

00:34.840 --> 00:38.800
In this thought experiment, our friend the cat is living in a world of uncertainty.

00:38.800 --> 00:43.400
A world where, if a radioactivity monitor detects any sign of radioactivity from the

00:43.400 --> 00:45.860
source, the flask shatters.

00:45.860 --> 00:50.440
The poison within the flask is then released, tragically ending our cat's life.

00:50.440 --> 00:55.320
But until we open the box, we have absolutely no idea if the cat is alive or dead.

00:55.320 --> 01:00.360
So, in a way, it's like our feline friend is simultaneously both alive and dead.

01:00.360 --> 01:04.320
Hold on, how can something be both alive and dead at the same time, you might ask?

01:04.320 --> 01:07.400
Well, welcome to the world of quantum physics.

01:07.400 --> 01:12.560
It's a place where the conventional rules of nature, as we know them, take a back seat.

01:12.560 --> 01:17.480
In the quantum world, something can exist in all its possible states at once.

01:17.480 --> 01:20.600
That is, until we check, or measure it.

01:20.600 --> 01:24.720
It's only at the moment of checking, do we force nature to make a decision.

01:24.720 --> 01:29.120
So until we open that box, the cat is in a state of quantum superposition.

01:29.120 --> 01:30.860
It's both dead and alive.

01:30.860 --> 01:35.480
But the moment we open the box, reality collapses to one possibility.

01:35.480 --> 01:38.100
Either the cat will be alive or it will be dead.

01:38.100 --> 01:41.560
This might sound strange, but that's the way the quantum world works.

01:41.560 --> 01:45.320
So what does this all mean for our furry friend in the box?

01:45.320 --> 01:49.480
Well, Schrödinger's cat is more than a peculiar pet story.

01:49.480 --> 01:53.880
It's a vivid illustration of the mind-bending world of quantum physics.

01:53.880 --> 01:58.620
In this quantum realm, things can exist in several states simultaneously.

01:58.620 --> 02:02.120
Just like our cat, who is both alive and dead at the same time.

02:02.120 --> 02:03.560
Bizarre, isn't it?

02:03.560 --> 02:06.440
This is until we open the box and observe.

02:06.440 --> 02:11.000
It's at this point that the cat must choose to be either alive or dead.

02:11.000 --> 02:15.380
This might sound like a tall tale, but it's a fundamental principle in quantum mechanics.

02:15.380 --> 02:20.760
It's called superposition, the ability of particles to be in multiple places at once.

02:20.760 --> 02:22.640
Don't worry if this seems a bit strange.

02:22.640 --> 02:27.920
Quantum physics is notoriously tricky to wrap our heads around, but it's this very weirdness

02:27.920 --> 02:32.320
that makes our universe so fascinating and endlessly intriguing.

02:32.320 --> 02:36.280
So next time you see a closed box, remember, anything could be happening inside until you

02:36.280 --> 02:37.000
take a peek.

